# Workflow

```
Grill (when the design branches) → Issue → branch → work → PR → human review → squash merge → retrospective
```

1. Grill when the API, the meaning, or a milestone boundary branches. Skill: `.agents/skills/grilling/SKILL.md`. Skip it when the Issue already has acceptance text and the design settles the question.
2. One Issue is one PR. Create the Issue from a template in `.github/ISSUE_TEMPLATE/`. Blank issues are off.
3. A change to the meaning of the spec is a `design` Issue that updates `docs/design.ja.md` before any implementation Issue. The Japanese file is the spec.
4. Acceptance text lives on the Issue. The roadmap keeps ID, title, Issue, and status only.
5. Branch from `main`: `type/<issue-number>-<slug>`, for example `feat/12-pchip`. `type` is a Conventional Commits type (`.cursor/rules/conventional-commits.mdc`).
6. Open the PR in the same turn that the branch is pushed. Use `.github/pull_request_template.md` (`.cursor/rules/pull-requests.mdc`). Write `Closes #N` on its own line under `## Related` (`.cursor/rules/github-closing.mdc`).
7. A human reviews and squash-merges. An agent never merges.
8. After the merge, delete the local and remote branch. A withdrawn prototype's branch is not a merged branch: post its diff on the Issue and leave the branch until the owner names it.
9. Retrospective, below, before the next feature branch.

## Gates

- `just lint`: `cargo fmt --check` and `cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings`.
  - CI lints with the newest stable toolchain, and a new clippy brings new lints. Before a PR is opened, `rustup update stable`, and say in the PR which `clippy --version` passed. In #129 the local toolchain was two releases behind (1.97 against 1.99): `just lint` passed here and `lint (stable)` was red in CI on a lint the older clippy did not have, and the PR said only that `just lint` passed.
  - The Rust packages under `compare/perf/runners/` are not in `just lint`. When a PR touches one, run `cargo fmt` and `cargo clippy -- -D warnings` in it.
- `just test`: `cargo test`. It never runs Python. Golden data is read from `compare/goldens/`.
- `just coverage`: line coverage of at least 90% (`cargo-llvm-cov`). Raising the threshold is a new Issue.
  - The table counts a generic function by the one instantiation that ran the most of its lines, not by all of them together. A function whose instantiations run different lines (a branch for zero dimensions) shows a missed line though every line ran. When the table and the per-line report (`cargo llvm-cov report --lcov`) disagree, compute which line it is from the exported data (`--json`) before saying why (`.cursor/rules/open-ends.mdc`). A `?` that never runs shows up the same way, and is a finding (`.cursor/rules/types.mdc`).
- `just bench` is not a gate. CI does not run criterion.

CI (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs lint, tests on three OSes with `--no-default-features` and `--all-features`, the MSRV job, and coverage. After each merge, read `main`'s CI. A red `main` gets its own Issue and PR before the next merge.

## Forbidden

- Starting implementation without an Issue number and a branch.
- Copying acceptance text onto the roadmap.
- Starting a roadmap row that reads `Set after Grill on #n`.
- Adding or removing roadmap rows, or touching **Intentionally out of scope**, without a Grill.
- Merging. Closing an Issue or PR without an imperative that names it.
- Ending a turn on a branch that has no Issue and no PR, or leaving files you created that are no longer needed (`.cursor/rules/scratch.mdc`).

## Long commands and stacked PRs

A command expected to take more than a minute or two is announced with its expected time before it starts, or it runs in the background.

The roadmap's **Current work** is edited only by the PR that finishes the row it names, or by a PR whose Issue is to change the order. A rules PR and a side PR leave it alone: three open PRs each rewrote it in M3-5, and it conflicted three times. Two rows that edit the same lines (one function) are opened one after the other. A second Issue that builds on an open PR gets its own branch and PR based on the first. A stacked PR is merged only after its base has merged. When the base merges, retarget the next PR to `main` first, then delete the merged branch. After a stack has merged, compare `main` with the head of the last branch: a PR merged into a branch that had already merged does not reach `main`.

Create an Issue before its number is written anywhere: a branch name, a commit, a PR body, an ADR. Issue and pull request numbers share one sequence, and the next one is not predictable. A PR body is written after `gh issue create` has returned the number, and a file that names the PR's own number is committed after `gh pr create` has returned it. Twice a body was written with the number expected (#69 for #70, #76 for #77) and had to be corrected after the PR was open.

```text
# BAD — write `Closes #76` into the body, then create the Issue, which gets #77
# GOOD — create the Issue, read its number from the output, then write the body
```

## Language

Identifiers, rustdoc, README, and the `Cargo.toml` description are English. Issue bodies, PR bodies, and commit subjects may be Japanese. `type`, `scope`, and the PR template headings stay English. The spec is `docs/design.ja.md`. The English `docs/design.md` is a roadmap row before v0.1.0 and is not kept in sync until then.

Before calling a PR ready, and whenever the owner asks for the next step, read the reviews and inline comments of every open PR (`gh pr view <n> --json reviews,comments` and `gh api repos/{owner}/{repo}/pulls/<n>/comments`).

## Retrospective

After a squash merge, in the turn that learns of it, write three points as a comment on the merged PR. Write "none" when a point is empty.

1. A lesson for `.cursor/rules/`, a skill, `docs/design.ja.md`, or one ADR under `docs/adr/`. Tacit knowledge the work relied on, which nothing states yet, is a lesson: a way of measuring, a way a tool fails on this setup, a mistake the review or the owner had to catch.
2. The Issue that will carry it, if any.
3. Whether to refactor. Yes opens a `refactor` Issue that is next, before the next feature. Postponing it needs a roadmap row that names when (`.cursor/rules/defer.mdc`).

Then, without waiting for an ask, file a `chore` Issue and one PR that writes each lesson of point 1 into the file it names. One Issue and PR may carry the lessons of several retrospectives (a design PR and the feature PR stacked on it are usually one). The next feature branch waits until that PR is open. A lesson that changes what the library means goes through a `design` Issue instead.

There is no retrospective log: the comment on the PR and the rule are the record.

The first Grill on these rules left this step out, on the agent's proposal that lessons would be written into the rules when they were noticed. From M1-5 to M2-2 none was, and a correction from the owner went into the agent's memory instead of a rule. The step is here so that the question is asked after every merge.

```text
# BAD — the PR merged; the next row starts; what the row taught stays in the conversation
# GOOD — the PR merged; three points on it; a chore PR with the lessons is open; then the next row
```

## Lessons

A correction given in conversation lands in the rule it belongs to in the same change (`.cursor/rules/record-corrections.mdc`). A decision that needs its reason kept becomes an ADR in `docs/adr/` (Status, Date, Issue, Context, Decision).
